 Democratic-sponsored May survey, showed Grothman ahead by twenty-two points.", "watch": "A late surge in Democratic spending or outside money targeting the district, combined with a strong national Democratic environment, could narrow the margin enough to move this toward Likely R.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Glenn Grothman (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican incumbent Glenn Grothman seeks another term in a central Wisconsin district that leans about eight points Republican at baseline. He faces Democrat Brad Smith, a first-time nominee who won the August primary and picked up the state AFL-CIO endorsement, along with minor-party and independent candidates. Grothman has raised roughly $929,000 to Smith's $166,000 and holds a wide cash advantage, and he has stayed visible with constituent-focused events, including a disaster declaration request and a hearing on oral cancer treatment costs.\n\nEvery major race rater places this in Safe or Solid Republican territory, and prediction markets give Smith only a small chance. The lone available poll, a Democratic-sponsored May survey of likely voters, showed Grothman up twenty-two points. With no credible general-election polling and a low-profile challenger, the race is expected to stay comfortably in Republican hands, though the margin could tighten somewhat in a strong Democratic year.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0556, "margin": -10.42}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0454, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0701, "margin": -11.0}]}